text:
What you will be able to do
By the end of this part you should be able to do each of the following on paper:

derive exponential tail bounds for sums of independent variables, and for normal and Laplace noise;
compute the Kullback–Leibler (KL), Rényi, total-variation and hockey-stick divergences between two normal or two Laplace distributions, and explain why processing data cannot increase any of them;
derive the optimal test between two distributions and its trade-off curve between the two kinds of error;
prove convergence rates for stochastic gradient descent, and bound how far a regularized minimizer moves when one training record is replaced;
prove that a uniformly stable algorithm generalizes;
compute entropy, mutual information and min-entropy leakage, and say when the last two disagree;
state a security definition as a game between two worlds, and prove a simple scheme secure with a hybrid argument or a simulator.

Each of these is a result derived in one of the chapters that follow.

What you will be able to do
By the end of this part you should be able to:

state any privacy attack as a game, name its threat model and its baseline, and bound its advantage by a total variation distance;
derive the loss-threshold membership attack from the generalization gap and LiRA from the Neyman–Pearson lemma, and evaluate both by the TPR at a low FPR;
separate attribute inference from what the population already reveals, and say why masking confidences does not stop it;
explain how a property of the training population can leak when no individual record does;
define verbatim, discoverable and extractable memorization, and compute a canary’s exposure;
recover an input exactly from the gradient of a fully connected layer, and say why averaging a batch does not remove the leak;
invert a hashed identifier by enumeration, compute the fraction of a population made unique by a set of quasi-identifiers, and run a Netflix-style de-anonymization;
say what k-anonymity, l-diversity and t-closeness each guarantee, and break each one with homogeneity, skewness and composition attacks;
prove that answers with error \(o(\sqrt n)\) to random subset sums allow almost all of a dataset to be reconstructed, and predict how many queries an attack on a deployed query system needs.

Each of these is a result derived in one of the chapters that follow.

What you will be able to do
By the end of this part you should be able to:

state pure and approximate DP for a stated neighbouring relation and unit of privacy, and translate a guarantee into a bound on membership advantage and on posterior odds;
prove post-processing, group privacy and basic composition, and say what does not compose;
calibrate the Laplace and Gaussian mechanisms, including the exact (analytic) Gaussian calibration;
derive advanced composition and compute optimal composition exactly;
prove the exponential mechanism, report-noisy-max and the sparse vector technique private, and recognize the common broken variants of the last;
handle data-dependent sensitivity with smooth sensitivity, propose-test-release, subsample-and-aggregate and clipping;
implement discrete Laplace and discrete Gaussian samplers exactly, and explain why naive floating-point noise is not private;
design frequency estimation in the local model and explain what shuffling and secure aggregation buy;
prove packing and fingerprinting lower bounds, and run the tracing attack they come from.

Each of these is a result derived in one of the chapters that follow.

What you will be able to do
By the end of this part you should be able to:

compute the RDP curve of the Gaussian, Laplace and randomized-response mechanisms, compose them, and convert the result to \((\varepsilon, \delta)\) with both the classic and the improved conversion;
explain what an RDP curve, a zCDP parameter and a trade-off function each keep and lose about the privacy loss;
derive privacy amplification by subsampling, and compute the RDP of the Poisson-subsampled Gaussian;
build a privacy-loss-distribution accountant with pessimistic and optimistic error bounds, and use it to check any other accountant;
say when shuffling or hiding intermediate iterates gives extra privacy, and when it does not apply;
run a privacy filter, and compute per-record privacy spend in DP-SGD;
pick an accountant for a DP-SGD run, state its assumptions, and report \(\varepsilon\) in a way that someone else can check.

What you will be able to do
By the end of this part you should be able to:

train a convex model privately by output, objective or gradient perturbation, and predict its excess risk;
implement DP-SGD from scratch, with per-example clipping, Poisson sampling and an RDP accountant, and explain what each hyperparameter trades;
account for hyperparameter search, and run a private selection procedure;
explain when DP-FTRL or a matrix-factorization mechanism beats DP-SGD, and why;
train a teacher ensemble and answer queries with PATE or a private in-context learner;
decide whether to fine-tune all parameters or a few, with a public pretrained model;
account for user-level privacy, and bound each user’s contribution;
measure and explain the disparate impact of private training on small groups;
choose an \(\varepsilon\) and justify it to someone who will hold you to it;
audit a private training run with canaries and turn the attack’s success into an empirical lower bound on \(\varepsilon\);
explain why a reused holdout set overfits, size a holdout set for adaptively chosen questions, and reuse one safely with Gaussian noise or Thresholdout.
